{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And in your majesty</b> — lacking a bet, like <i>ki sheshet yamim asah</i> (Exodus 20:11), as though it said: and in your majesty prosper. Like the sense of <i>lo yitslach be-yamav</i> (Daniel 11:17). The meaning: he shall prosper when he rides. Some say: <i>tselach</i> is from the root of <i>pen yitslach ka-esh</i> (Exodus 22:5). And behold, it recounts together with his might his faithfulness and his humility and his righteousness." "<b>And the phrase 'of meekness righteousness'</b> — it stands both independent and in construct, and the meaning is: meekness along with righteousness." "<b>Your thigh</b> — your right hand. Both the agent and the action are lacking, as though it were written: your right hand teaches you to perform mighty and awesome acts of war, in an exalted mode of speaking about you. Rabbi Yosi said: <i>vetorcha</i> is from the root of <i>viyorem Elohim</i> (Deuteronomy 32:27), and the letter after it is written: <i>chitzchak chitsim</i> (Psalms 45:6). But it would be fitting, were it not that <i>viyorem</i> connotes for them disparagement, while this connotes praise."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {2 "Ibn Ezra puns on the root ירה/ירא; his argument turns on the ambiguity of the surrounding letters. 'Stands both independent and in construct' refers to whether ענוה is pausal or construct."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:30:59.216553362Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}